A||||6||gabriel||protecting the rotors||||1||
Z||000000||gabriel||03-30-2001||01:39 PM||||Hi.<P> I've done my own upgrade on the braking system. This upgrade uses the front rotors from the PT cruiser on the Fiero rear. - Along with the rear calipers from the Camero, this system allows the retaining of the parking brake feature.<P> My question:<P> The car is not being driven & the rotors are beginning to rust.  What has anyone used to protect the rotors when the car is inactive that won't screw up the pads when the car is driven?  <BR>   -Thanks.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||03-30-2001||02:22 PM||||Well, you could try a light coat of oil, but you'd need to clean the rotors before driving the car.  Unless the car will be stored for long duration (several months) I wouldn't worry about it.  The rust will come off as soon as you start driving, and it shouldn't hurt the pads much.  Anybody have any tips that they use when storing their car for the winter?  ||||reg||3
Z||000002||Mr.Frosty||03-30-2001||02:49 PM||||I've got a question along those lines.Does anybody know of a way to keep the overall surface of the rotors from rusting?Can you use a high temp paint or something?I hate the look of rusting rotors "UGLY".<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top <BR>85 GT Turbo||||reg||5
Z||000003||Tigger||03-30-2001||03:34 PM||||Some automotive manufactures use a coating to protect the surface of the rotors, usually on luxury or high priced cars, just so they don't rust during the trip to dealers and so customers don't say "ewe yuk... look rusty rotors... is that safe?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">".  The coating wears off after around 30 stops.  I'm not sure what the coating is or if you can buy it off the shelf, I just know it's out there.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||03-30-2001||07:56 PM||||I WOULDNT risk putting anything on braking surface. You might help in storage by buying a cheap set of vinyl wheel cover from an auto body/paint supply store. ($20) Like they say, a few stops will clean them off. My van sits for days at a time and after a rain they tend to lock up the first few times from rust (alloy wheels) but then there fine. I also make sure i drive the car a little ways after washing it to make sure I dry the brakes out. Any kind of lube will ruin the brake pads. Dont know if this will work but maybe a coat of clear laquer is what the dealers put on them, but I wouldnt myself.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 03-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bill Osmer||03-30-2001||08:10 PM||||gabriel -<P>This sounds like an interesting upgrade. Why don't you write more about your experience (add pictures, too). Everyone has heard about the Berretta upgrade, but yours sounds like a novel alternative.||||reg||5
Z||000006||theogre||03-30-2001||09:15 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I want all the details of that upgrade for evaluation for the brake upgrade article. I need every scrap of info you have.<P><B>NEVER NEVER NEVER NEVER</B> put oil of any kind on a brake parts. Petrolium products and brake parts DO NOT coexist peacefully.<P>The brake pads are made to deal with a certain amount of rust on the rotors. how much exactly varies for product to product. <P>Thing one in storing a car is to eliminate direct paths for water to spray on the rotors durring rain etc. The wheel cover idea above is good. You could also use brake dust shields the same way. Another posibility is to drape plastic over the wheel and brake parts. Tie the plastic to the upper A arm or strut and let it cover over the whole assembly.<P><BR>As for appearance thru AL wheels.... you could powder coat the hat. That would look nice and can be done in any color you want. You could also ask <A HREF="http://www.techlinecoatings.com/" TARGET=_blank>Tech Line Coatings</A> if they have something that would prevent rust on them. You'd probably have to back it on.||||reg||1
